1
00:00:00,360 --> 00:00:04,600
Module 3, Video 3.4, Screens Officers Can Use

2
00:00:05,240 --> 00:00:10,020
An officer never types what the system already knows and never chooses from an endless list.

3
00:00:10,840 --> 00:00:17,660
So, uh, thinking about that, why does a seemingly small waste on an internal screen even matter to a ministry's operations?

4
00:00:18,080 --> 00:00:19,780
Well, it really comes down to the frequency.

5
00:00:20,040 --> 00:00:24,040
I mean, an applicant uses a self-service portal, you know, maybe once or twice.

6
00:00:24,300 --> 00:00:25,120
Right. Just occasionally.

7
00:00:25,300 --> 00:00:28,840
Exactly. But an officer uses their screens many times a day.

8
00:00:29,160 --> 00:00:36,380
Oh, wow. Yeah. So a small waste is repeated thousands of times a year, and, uh, every single value typed again is a chance for a mistake.

9
00:00:36,760 --> 00:00:37,860
Which really adds up for

10
00:00:38,500 --> 00:00:40,020
the official running these systems.

11
00:00:40,260 --> 00:00:41,100
It definitely does.

12
00:00:41,820 --> 00:00:41,940
So

13
00:00:42,600 --> 00:00:48,460
how do we prevent screens from just becoming like public web forms where everything is typed in free text?

14
00:00:49,220 --> 00:00:51,680
By applying the five rules of the SDD method.

15
00:00:52,000 --> 00:00:52,760
I'll number them aloud.

16
00:00:53,400 --> 00:00:55,940
One, never type what the system already knows.

17
00:00:56,360 --> 00:00:57,200
Okay. Makes sense.

18
00:00:57,760 --> 00:00:59,780
Two, never choose from an endless list.

19
00:01:00,540 --> 00:01:03,140
Three, choose a decision from the decisions allowed.

20
00:01:03,340 --> 00:01:04,780
Do not write it as free text.

21
00:01:05,180 --> 00:01:06,520
Right. No free text decisions.

22
00:01:07,320 --> 00:01:10,280
Four, state on the screen the rule that governs the work.

23
00:01:11,200 --> 00:01:13,780
And five, show an open question with its owner.

24
00:01:14,180 --> 00:01:15,420
Never answer it with a guess.

25
00:01:16,080 --> 00:01:23,820
Let's ground this a bit. What does an initial unoptimized screen look like, say, for a PHEQA inspection visit in

26
00:01:24,120 --> 00:01:24,620
Progressa?

27
00:01:25,400 --> 00:01:28,860
And, uh, Progressa is purely the fictional country of these examples.

28
00:01:29,480 --> 00:01:30,240
Right. Of course.

29
00:01:30,840 --> 00:01:37,340
So imagine an AI-drafted screen. The inspector has to pick from a list of all two hundred and fourteen institutions.

30
00:01:37,900 --> 00:01:40,760
Wow. That is a massive list to scroll through.

31
00:01:41,400 --> 00:01:44,340
It really is. Then she types the address manually.

32
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:46,480
Wait, even if it's already known?

33
00:01:47,100 --> 00:01:48,060
Yes, manually.

34
00:01:48,800 --> 00:01:52,440
After that, she writes the outcome as a completely free text sentence.

35
00:01:52,800 --> 00:01:56,260
Plus, there's a box arbitrarily requiring two signatures with no source.

36
00:01:56,780 --> 00:01:57,640
That sounds

37
00:01:58,420 --> 00:01:59,480
highly prone to errors.

38
00:02:00,220 --> 00:02:03,960
It is. And to top it off, she can save a visit on literally any date.

39
00:02:04,720 --> 00:02:07,640
Okay, so how do the first four rules fix this draft?

40
00:02:08,440 --> 00:02:13,180
Well, instead of scrolling, the officer just types part of the register number to pick from a few matches.

41
00:02:13,660 --> 00:02:15,180
Huh, skipping that huge list.

42
00:02:15,660 --> 00:02:18,960
Yes. Second, the address autofills from the register.

43
00:02:19,740 --> 00:02:20,140
Much better.

44
00:02:20,880 --> 00:02:25,500
Third, the outcome is restricted to three specific choices, though there is a box for findings.

45
00:02:26,000 --> 00:02:26,340
Got it.

46
00:02:27,260 --> 00:02:28,020
And what about the date?

47
00:02:28,420 --> 00:02:33,760
A line right under the date states the rule that a recommendation must rest on a visit made within ninety days.

48
00:02:34,260 --> 00:02:35,320
That covers the first four.

49
00:02:36,040 --> 00:02:40,640
How does the fifth rule resolve the AI's guess that, uh, two inspectors must sign?

50
00:02:41,220 --> 00:02:47,960
Well, the regulations actually gave no specific number, so now the field states, "To be confirmed by the Registrar of PHEQA."

51
00:02:48,700 --> 00:02:51,300
Oh, I see. So it clearly assigns an owner.

52
00:02:51,740 --> 00:02:57,780
Exactly. The screen's design cannot be accepted while that mark stands, so the question reached the registrar who answered it.

53
00:02:58,220 --> 00:02:59,200
And what was the answer?

54
00:02:59,900 --> 00:03:01,880
One inspector and the head of the inspectorate.

55
00:03:02,800 --> 00:03:05,480
What did an actual inspector say about these changes?

56
00:03:06,040 --> 00:03:10,540
She confirmed that retyping addresses or scrolling long lists directly causes errors.

57
00:03:11,760 --> 00:03:13,580
I can completely see why.

58
00:03:13,800 --> 00:03:18,660
Yeah. So my advice, before you accept a screen in your program, sit beside an officer.

59
00:03:19,800 --> 00:03:20,940
Just to observe how they work.

60
00:03:21,380 --> 00:03:24,780
Right. Count the values she types that the system already holds.

61
00:03:25,560 --> 00:03:29,760
Okay. Count the lists she scrolls and the decisions she writes in her own words.

62
00:03:30,820 --> 00:03:33,240
That is a highly practical way to measure it.

63
00:03:33,780 --> 00:03:37,760
Count what an officer types that the system already knows and every list she scrolls.

64
00:03:38,060 --> 00:03:41,040
Each one is a rule not followed and a mistake waiting to happen.
